.\" $Revision: 1.0 $
.TH PGPVERIFY 8
.SH NAME
pgpverify \- verify the PGP signature of a control message
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pgpverify
[
.B \-d[level]
]
[
.B \-k
.I keyring
]
[
.I program
[
.I args
] ]
<
.I article
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pgpverify
reads a news article on standard input, rebuilds the text signed by its
.B X-PGP-Sig
header (as created by signcontrol) and checks the signature.
On success it prints the first word of the signer's user id, for example
.BR news.announce.newgroups ,
and exits 0.
.B dreaderd
runs it for control messages that dcontrol.ctl marks
.BR verify-<signer> ,
with the arguments from
.B pgpverifyargs
in diablo.config.
.PP
By default GnuPG's
.B gpgv
(1.x or 2.x) is used.  It is looked for as gpgv2, gpgv and gpgv1 in
/usr/bin, /usr/local/bin, /opt/local/bin, /usr/pkg/bin and /opt/ooce/bin.
A
.I program
named
.B pgp
is run as PGP 2.x ("pgp \-f") instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-k keyring
The keyring to trust (a relative name is taken from the current
directory).  Default: path_home/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg if it exists,
else gpgv's default in ~news/.gnupg.  It must be in the classic keyring
format; gpgv 1.x cannot read a keybox (.kbx).
.TP
.B \-d[level]
Show the verifier command and its status output on standard error.  Level 2
also keeps the temporary files pgpverify.msg.* and pgpverify.sig.* in
$TMPDIR (default /tmp).
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 good signature; 1 no X-PGP-Sig header; 2 malformed header or verifier not
runnable; 3 bad signature or signer key not in the keyring.
.SH FILES
/news/pgp/trustedkeys.gpg
.SH "SEE ALSO"
dreaderd(8),
diablo-files(5),
README.PGP for the keyring setup with examples
